From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 18 Aug 2026 01:10:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 28/28] build: let the m68k-emu68 target keep frame pointers

Diagnostics are an exception to the performance work, and until now this
target could not make that exception: it hard-coded CFLAGS_OMIT_FP.

KrnBacktraceFromFrame() walks the chain GCC builds with link.w/unlk. With
frame pointers omitted there is no chain, so a backtrace stops at the frame it
was handed -- and the frame worth seeing is never in the module that detected
the fault. The heap-corruption reporter in rom/kernel/tlsf.c names the block
and the task and then has nothing to say about who freed it.

aarch64, x86_64 and riscv64 all append CFLAGS_NO_OMIT_FP unconditionally for
exactly this reason. m68k gets a switch rather than an unconditional flag,
because here the cost is paid twice: %a6 is a register the JIT would rather
have, and this target is built for size.

    BELLATRIX_FRAME_POINTERS=1 ./scripts/build-aros.sh

The variable is read at configure time and therefore applies to the whole
target uniformly -- which is the point, since the interesting caller is in a
different module from the detector.

scripts/build-aros.sh defaults it to 1, so a development build of this
repository keeps frame pointers and only an explicit 0 gives them up. That
default is the caller's policy and not this patch's: the branch here honours
whatever it is told, and an unset variable still means what upstream means.
---
--- a/configure
+++ b/configure
@@ -11350,8 +11350,24 @@
                 aros_isa_flags="$""(ISA_MC68040_FLAGS)"
                 aros_target_mkdep="$aros_target_mkdep -D__GNUC__ -D__mc68000__ -D__EMU68__"
                 aros_config_cppflags="$aros_config_cppflags -D__EMU68__"
-                aros_config_cflags="$aros_config_cflags $""(CFLAGS_NO_STRICT_ALIASING) $""(CFLAGS_FREESTANDING) $""(CFLAGS_OMIT_FP) $""(CFLAGS_BUILTIN)"
-                aros_config_cxxflags="$aros_config_cxxflags $""(CFLAGS_NO_STRICT_ALIASING) $""(CFLAGS_OMIT_FP) $""(CFLAGS_BUILTIN)"
+                # Frame pointers are omitted by default: %a6 is a register the
+                # JIT would rather have, and this target is size-optimised.
+                #
+                # BELLATRIX_FRAME_POINTERS=1 keeps them instead, for the whole
+                # target. KrnBacktraceFromFrame() walks the link/unlk chain, so
+                # without this every backtrace stops at the frame it was handed
+                # -- and the frame worth seeing is always in some other module
+                # than the one that detected the fault. aarch64, x86_64 and
+                # riscv64 all pass CFLAGS_NO_OMIT_FP unconditionally for the
+                # same reason; m68k makes it a switch because here it is paid
+                # in JITted code.
+                if test "x$BELLATRIX_FRAME_POINTERS" = "x1"; then
+                    aros_emu68_fp_cflags="$""(CFLAGS_NO_OMIT_FP)"
+                else
+                    aros_emu68_fp_cflags="$""(CFLAGS_OMIT_FP)"
+                fi
+                aros_config_cflags="$aros_config_cflags $""(CFLAGS_NO_STRICT_ALIASING) $""(CFLAGS_FREESTANDING) $aros_emu68_fp_cflags $""(CFLAGS_BUILTIN)"
+                aros_config_cxxflags="$aros_config_cxxflags $""(CFLAGS_NO_STRICT_ALIASING) $aros_emu68_fp_cflags $""(CFLAGS_BUILTIN)"
                 aros_target_strip_flags="-R.comment --strip-debug"
                 if test "$optimization" = "default"; then
                     optimization_level=$toolchain_size_opt_lvl
